module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulSelfExtExteriorIsomorphism

/-!
# The comparison map of Stacks 0BQY is an isomorphism, for a global Koszul presentation

Let `Y` be a ringed space, let `φ : (O_Y)ʳ ⟶ O_Y` have image the sheaf of ideals `𝓘`, write
`O_X = O_Y/𝓘`, and let `K` be a Koszul chain datum for `φ` whose augmentation is a
quasi-isomorphism — that is, let `𝓘` be generated by a Koszul-regular sequence `f₁, …, f_r` of
global sections. This file proves that composition makes

`⋀ⁱ_{O_X}(Ext¹_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)) ⟶ Extⁱ_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)`

an isomorphism for every `i : ℤ`.

The three degree ranges are settled by three different arguments. Below zero both sides vanish.
In degree zero both sides are free of rank one on their units, and a homomorphism of graded
algebras preserves the unit. Above zero isomorphy is checked on stalks, where the map becomes the
duality pairing between the `i`-th exterior power of a free module of rank `r` and the `i`-th
exterior power of its dual, which is bijective.

The positive-degree computation joins two halves. The *cocycle* half says that the composition
product of `i` degree-one coordinate-contraction classes is the class of the `i`-fold iterated
contraction; the *stalk* half says that the iterated contraction at a stalk is the iterated
contraction against the corresponding coordinate projections, hence a determinant. What is done
here is to put them on the same side of every identification — the stalk of the source, the stalk
of the target, and the change of base ring from the stalk of `O_Y` to the stalk of `O_X` — and to
read the result off with `Koszul.bijective_of_listContractionDual_proj_rev`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.isIso_extComparison_forall`: **the comparison is an
  isomorphism in every degree.**
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.isExteriorAlgebra_extSelfAlgebra`: equivalently, the
  self-Ext algebra is an exterior algebra on its degree-one piece.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.extComparisonStalkDual_ιMulti_proj`: the local
  computation in one statement — in coordinates at a stalk, the comparison carries a wedge of
  coordinate projections to the iterated contraction against them.
* `KoszulChainData.extSelfAlgebraStalkDualEquiv_germAt_iteratedContractionClassOn`: the target
  half — the germ of the class of an iterated contraction is that iterated contraction,
  over the stalk of `O_X`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.homologyPieceStalkEquiv_germ_iteratedContraction`: the
  germ formula with its three degree transports removed.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.isIso_extComparison_of_pos` and
  `isIso_extComparison_zero` (in `KoszulSelfExtExteriorIsomorphism`) and
  `isIso_extComparison_of_neg`: the three degree ranges.

## Implementation notes

**The declarations here are not registered under [Stacks, Tag 0BQY], the hypothesis here being
stronger than the tag's.** The tag is registered on
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isIso_desc_app_isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf` in
`KoszulSelfExtKoszulRegular`; its local step, at the tag's own hypothesis, is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.forall_isIso_extComparison_of_isKoszulSequenceExactOn`
in `KoszulSelfExtExteriorLocal`; what is below assumes more.
[Stacks, Tag 0BQY] assumes only that `𝓘` is Koszul-regular, which is a *local*
condition: its proof opens with "to see this is true we may work locally on `Y`; hence we may
assume that we have global sections `f₁, …, f_r` of `O_Y` which generate `𝓘` and which form a
Koszul regular sequence." Everything after that sentence is what is proved here; the reduction
itself needs the self-Ext algebra and its comparison map defined intrinsically from
composition on `RSheafHom_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)`, independently of a Koszul model, together with the
agreement of that intrinsic comparison with the one established over an open carrying a Koszul
datum. That is carried out elsewhere, over the composition-product algebra of a K-injective
resolution of the quotient, and the tag's global statement is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isIso_desc_app_isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulSelfExtKoszulRegular`.

**Transports are moved by `refine`/`Eq.trans`, never by `rw`.** Two separate obstructions. The
indexing of the Koszul cochain complex makes `(asCochain K.chain).X n` and `K.chain.X (-n)` the
same object definitionally and not syntactically, and `rw` matches at `instances` transparency,
where `asCochain` does not unfold. Worse, `rw` cannot even build its motive here: the two
spellings `TopCat.Sheaf CommRingCat X` and `Sheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) CommRingCat` of
the sheaf of rings make `kabstract` produce an ill-typed motive, reported as "did not find an
occurrence of the pattern". `refine` and `Eq.trans` elaborate at default transparency and are
immune to both.

**The stalk of a sheaf of modules must be spelled `modStalk`.** The `O_x`-module structure on it is
found by instance search only at that spelling; at the unfolded
`TopCat.Presheaf.stalk M.val.presheaf x` it is not, recognising the ring needing higher-order
unification. This is the issue documented at `Stacks.SheafOfModules.SheafHomStalk`, and it is why
`extComparisonStalkSourceEquiv` ascribes its target type.

**The contracting order is reversed, and the reversal is a genuine sign.** The composition product
of `c_{j₀}, …, c_{jᵢ}` in that order is the class of `δ_{jᵢ} ∘ ⋯ ∘ δ_{j₀}`, because a cochain of
the internal endomorphism complex composes its factors in the opposite order. Reversing the list
permutes the rows of the Kronecker matrix whose determinant the pairing is, so the comparison
differs from `exteriorPower.pairingDual` by `(-1)^{i(i-1)/2}`. That is a unit, so bijectivity is
unaffected; `Koszul.bijective_of_listContractionDual_proj_rev` is the form of the recognition
criterion that absorbs it.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BQY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BQY)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

